1.1.text:
PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE.
CHAPTER I
GENERAL PROVISIONS
Article 1
In this Regional Regulation, what is meant by:
1. Region is the Regency of West Southeast Maluku;
2. Regional Government is the Regent and Regional Apparatus as elements of regional government administration;
3. Regent is the Regent of West Southeast Maluku;
4. Regional People's Representative Council hereinafter referred to as DPRD is the Regional People's Representative Council of West Southeast Maluku Regency;
5. Child is a person who has not yet reached the age of 18 (eighteen) years, including a child still in the womb;
6. Woman is every person who is physically born as a woman;
7. Victim is a woman and child who experiences acts of violence and/or threats of violence that include physical, sexual, psychological, economic, and/or social violence, in the family, community and/or society, in conflict situations, post-conflict and state;
8. Implementation is all actions that include protection, services, and recovery for victims of violence;
9. Protection is all efforts aimed at providing a sense of security to victims carried out by the regional government, family parties, advocates, social institutions, society, police, prosecutors, courts or other parties;
10. Child Protection is all activities carried out to guarantee and protect children and their rights so that they can live, grow, develop, and participate optimally in accordance with human rights and dignity, and receive protection from violence and discrimination;
11. Women's Protection is all activities carried out to guarantee and protect women and their rights so that they can live, grow, develop, and participate optimally in accordance with human rights and dignity, and receive protection from violence and discrimination;
12. Service is action taken as soon as possible to victims, when seeing, hearing and knowing of, during or after the occurrence of violence against victims;
13. Companion is a person or institution that has the ability to provide assistance to victims to conduct counseling, therapy, legal services and advocacy, for the purpose of strengthening victim recovery;
14. Women and Child Protection Institution is an institution providing services to victims of violence, based in hospitals, managed jointly in the form of medical services (including medicolegal), psycho-social and legal services;
15. Safe House is temporary housing used to provide protection for women and children victims in accordance with the standard operating procedures or abbreviated as SOP that are determined;
16. Society is individuals, families, groups, social organizations and or other community organizations;
17. Family is the smallest unit in society consisting of husband-wife and their children, or father and his children, and mother and her children;
18. Household is family members and relatives such as grandchildren, nieces/nephews, older siblings, younger siblings, grandfathers, grandmothers, husbands, wives, cousins and so on and non-relatives such as domestic workers, drivers and so on who live and reside in one house.

CHAPTER IV
OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES
Article 7
The implementation of protection for women and children is the joint obligation and responsibility of:
a. Regional Government;
b. Individuals;
c. Family;
d. Parents/Guardians;
e. Community;
f. Social Organizations; and or
g. Other Community Organizations in accordance with the provisions of applicable laws and regulations.
Article 8 The obligations and responsibilities of regional government as referred to in article 7 letter a include:
a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
b. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection for women and children from victims of violence;
c. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection for women and children from victims of violence;
d. allocating budget for the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity; and e. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence.
Article 9 (1) The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 7 letter e, are implemented in the form of community participation including: a. caring for, maintaining, educating, and protecting children;
•
b. developing children according to their abilities, talents and interests;
c. preventing marriage at child age;
d. preventing acts of violence against women and children;
e. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against women and children victims of violence to law enforcement or authorized parties; and
f. participating in handling victims of acts of violence.
(2) In the event that parents are absent, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a certain reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), may be transferred to the head of the family, which is implemented in accordance with the provisions of applicable Laws and Regulations;
(3) The form of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented responsibly in accordance with Laws and Regulations.

CHAPTER VII
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 16 (1) The community participates in the protection of children and women victims of violence individually or in groups.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, child protection institutions, social welfare institutions, community organizations, educational institutions, mass media, and the business sector.
(3) Community participation in the implementation of protection for children and women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by:
a. Providing information through socialization and education;
b. Providing input in the formulation of policies related to the protection of children and women victims of violence;
c. Reporting to the authorities if violations of the rights of children and women victims of violence occur;
d. Playing an active role in the rehabilitation and social reintegration process for children and women victims of violence;
e. Conducting monitoring, supervision, and sharing responsibility for the implementation of protection for children and women victims of violence;
f. Playing an active role in eliminating negative labeling of children and women victims of violence; and
g. Providing space for children and women victims of violence to participate and express their opinions.
(4) The role of community organizations and educational institutions as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by taking necessary steps in accordance with their respective duties, functions, and authorities to assist in the implementation for children and women victims of violence.
(5) The role of mass media as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out through the dissemination of information and educational materials that are beneficial from social, cultural, educational, religious, and health aspects for children and women victims of violence by taking into account the interests of children and women victims of violence.
(6) The role of the business sector as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out through:
a. Company policies that have a perspective on children and women victims of violence;
b. Products intended for children and women victims of violence must be safe for children and women victims of violence;
c. Contributing to the fulfillment of the rights of children and women victims of violence through corporate social responsibility.

Article 5
(1) In the event that victims of violence as referred to in article 4 require continuous handling, they are entitled to stay in a safe house or alternative residence;
(2) Continuous handling as referred to in paragraph (1) constitutes handling at the rehabilitation stage carried out either by individuals, groups or government and non-government institutions.

REGENT OF WEST SOUTHEAST MALUKU
MALUKU PROVINCE
REGIONAL REGULATION
OF WEST SOUTHEAST MALUKU REGENCY
NUMBER 04 YEAR 2017
CONCERNING
THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION
FOR WOMEN AND CHILD VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE
BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD
THE REGENT OF WEST SOUTHEAST MALUKU,

Considering: a. that as citizens, women and children have the right to obtain protection from any acts of violence committed against them that may cause physical or psychological harm, as part of the recognition and enforcement of Human Rights;
b. that the number of acts of violence against women and children in West Southeast Maluku Regency is quite high, while protection and services have not been carried out optimally;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Child Victims of Violence.

In View of: 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 4 Year 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1979, Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
3. Law Number 7 Year 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention On The Elimination Of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984, Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3668);
5. Law Number 39 Year 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
6. Law Number 46 Year 1999 concerning the Establishment of North Maluku Province, Buru Regency and West Southeast Maluku Regency (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 175, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3895); as amended by Law Number 06 Year 2000 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2000 Number 75, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3961);
•
7. Law Number 23 Year 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
8. Law Number 17 Year 2003 concerning State Finance (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2003 Number 47, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4286);
9. Law Number 23 year 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
10. Law Number 33 Year 2004 concerning Financial Balance Between the Central Government and Regional Governments (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 126, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4438);
11. Law Number 21 Year 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Human Trafficking (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007, Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
12. Law Number 12 Year 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
13. Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587);
14. Government Regulation Number 25 Year 2000 concerning the Authority of the Government and the Authority of the Province as an Autonomous Region (State Gazette Year 2000 Number 54, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 3952);
15. Government Regulation Number 38 Year 2007 concerning the Division of Government Affairs Between the Government, Provincial Regional Government and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 Year 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 Year 2008 concerning the Implementation of Child Protection;
18. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 Year 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for Women and Child Victims of Violence;
19. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 Year 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
20. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 Year 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Child Victims of Violence;
21. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 Year 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence.

With Mutual Approval

Article 8
Paragraph (1) Letter a What is meant by establishing and implementing protection policies, programs and activities includes:
a. Collecting data and information about women and children victims of violence;
b. Providing education about anti-violence values towards women and children;
c. Conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence;
d. Conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence.
